INTERPRETATION

The exact hand-written parent supports the compositional two-stage Task-2
environment reliably at one request per epoch.

This independently confirms the candidate environment identified by LU-2I.

The earlier LU-2G failure is therefore not evidence that Task-2 composition
itself is beyond the developmental substrate.

The dominant problem was environment capacity:

three Task-2 requests per epoch created severe pre-SENSE congestion under the
inherited 32-epoch lifetime.

At L1 the queue disappears, repair integrity closes in all ten fresh worlds,
both streams remain functional, no request expires, and recovery behavior is
finite across all ten worlds.

DECISION

The L1 Task-2 environment is authorized as the baseline environment for a
separately preregistered learned-U transfer experiment.

Do not change the learned authority dose automatically.

If learned U is tested next, use the accepted 25-percent U blend lineage
unless a new preregistration explicitly justifies another authority level.

Do not reuse the LU-2K validation worlds for the learned-U comparison.

Use fresh paired worlds with common randomness and bind all manifests before
execution.

PLAIN-SPEAK INTERPRETATION

We now have a fair Task-2 test bed.

The hand-written organism handled ten completely new two-step-task worlds at
one job per epoch and passed every one.

Nothing expired.
No wrong answer was marked DONE.
Both directions kept working.
Repair integrity passed in all ten.

That tells us the earlier Task-2 trouble was not because the organism could
not understand the two-stage job.

We were simply feeding the service pipeline too much work for its inherited
32-epoch lifetime.

Now we can finally ask the question LU-2G originally wanted to answer:

does the 25-percent learned recruiter still work when the organism is given a
fair compositional Task-2 environment?

